---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

// Playground page for the constructed-language side project ("Information Machines").
// This is a personal, incubating creative project — NOT research. Keep it honest about
// status: there is no fixed lexicon/phonology/writing system and no results. It documents
// a design and a method (a grammar built around information operators, kept honest with a
// changelog and a test suite), and links to its playable spin-off, RELAY (/relay/).

const operators = [
  { op: "ADD", fn: "Put a new piece of information into the current state." },
  { op: "LINK", fn: "Relate it to what came before — cause, explanation, contrast, example." },
  { op: "DERIVE", fn: "Mark that this follows from information already present." },
  { op: "REVISE", fn: "Change something that was asserted earlier." },
  { op: "RETRACT", fn: "Withdraw a previous commitment." },
  { op: "QUERY", fn: "Point at a gap and ask for it to be filled." },
  { op: "PRESERVE", fn: "Deliberately keep several readings open, without choosing yet." },
];

const suite = [
  "Describe something that happened yesterday.",
  "Pass along a rumor you can’t confirm.",
  "Correct something you just said wrong.",
  "Write a recipe, or a set of instructions.",
  "Explain why something happened.",
  "Describe a dream.",
  "Give a stranger directions.",
  "State a rule, or a prohibition.",
  "Tell a joke that turns on an ambiguity.",
  "Say “I don’t know, but I know that you know.”",
  "Describe something that has no name yet.",
  "Compress a complicated story into three sentences.",
];
---

<Base
  title="Information Machines · Xiaochuan Li"
  description="A constructed language whose grammar is built around one job — how information enters, connects, changes, and leaves a conversation. A personal, incubating side project with a changelog and a test suite. No results; it is never finished."
  showProgress={true}
  toc={true}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/#playground" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Playground</a></p>
    <div class="im-kicker">Playground · Constructed language</div>
    <h1>Information Machines</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-3">
      What would a language look like if the way information <em>enters, connects, changes, and
      leaves</em> a conversation were written directly into its grammar?
    </p>

    <div class="im-ops" aria-hidden="true">
      {operators.slice(0, 5).map((o) => <span class="im-op-chip">{o.op}</span>)}
    </div>

    <p>
      Information Machines is a small constructed language I build in my spare time. Most of my
      research approaches meaning from the outside — with analysis, diagnostics, and evidence. This
      is the inside-out version: rather than describe how some existing language handles information,
      I try to <em>design</em> one whose grammar is about nothing else, and watch what breaks. It
      doesn’t have to serve my research or defend a claim. It’s an object about language that I can
      actually run.
    </p>

    <h2>The operators</h2>
    <p>
      The first version doesn’t need a world, a vocabulary, or a sound system. It needs a handful of
      moves for changing an information state, and rules for combining them. These are the early
      candidates — the grammar’s verbs, so to speak:
    </p>
    <div class="im-grid">
      {operators.map((o) => (
        <div class="im-cell">
          <span class="im-op">{o.op}</span>
          <span class="im-fn">{o.fn}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p>
      A second axis is <strong>where information comes from</strong> — witnessed, reported, inferred,
      remembered, hypothesized, imagined. Whether the grammar should <em>force</em> you to mark the
      source, the way some natural languages do, is exactly the kind of question the project exists to
      play with. Nothing is decided in advance.
    </p>

    <h2>Unit tests for a language</h2>
    <p>
      The project doesn’t depend on anyone else adopting it, so it needs its own way of making
      progress. I borrow one from software: every version moves through the same loop.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Add or change <strong>one</strong> rule.</li>
      <li>Use it to express a fixed set of texts.</li>
      <li>Write down what can’t be said, comes out clumsy, or turns unexpectedly ambiguous.</li>
      <li>Revise the design — keeping the old version, and the reason for the change.</li>
      <li>Re-run the old tests: did the change break something that used to work?</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Over time that produces the real artifacts — a grammar, a growing dictionary, a corpus of test
      sentences, a changelog, a graveyard of abandoned rules, and the occasional short text written in
      the language itself.
    </p>

    <h2>The test suite</h2>
    <p>
      The tests are just things people routinely do with language. A rule earns its place only if the
      language can still say all of these afterward:
    </p>
    <ul class="im-suite">
      {suite.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h2>Where it stands</h2>
    <p class="im-status">
      It’s early. Right now Information Machines is a design and a method more than a finished
      language: there’s no fixed lexicon, phonology, or writing system, and there’s nothing here to
      report as a result. The first real move is small — pick one operator, write three rules and ten
      example sentences, and test them against a single real conversation. The point was never to
      finish. It’s to keep an object about language that can grow, fail, and be revised on the record.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rules that turn out to be fun to <em>discover</em>, rather than just to describe, get spun off
      into <a href="/relay/">RELAY</a> — a puzzle game built on the same idea, where you reverse-engineer
      a machine’s language instead of designing your own.
    </p>

    <p class="!mt-10 !text-sm !text-[var(--color-text-dim)]">
      <a href="/#playground" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Back to the Playground</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .im-kicker {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-dim);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  /* operator chips under the lede */
  .im-ops { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem; margin: 0 0 2rem; }
  .im-op-chip {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    color: #46567a;
    background: #eef1f7;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, #46567a 22%, transparent);
    border-radius: 999px;
    padding: 4px 11px;
  }

  /* operator grid */
  .im-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin: 1.4rem 0 1.6rem;
  }
  .im-cell {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.35rem;
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-left: 3px solid #f6ad28;
    border-radius: 0 9px 9px 0;
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }
  .im-op {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 12px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: #172137;
  }
  .im-fn { font-size: 0.9rem; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--color-text-muted); }

  .im-suite { columns: 2; column-gap: 2rem; }
  .im-suite li { break-inside: avoid; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }

  .im-status {
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text);
    padding: 1rem 1.2rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    background: var(--color-surface);
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .im-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .im-suite { columns: 1; }
  }
</style>
